WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.240
The Conjuring is the terrifying tale of one&nbsp;
family’s experience moving into a supposedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.240
haunted Rhode Island farmhouse, but before we&nbsp;
meet the family, we’re introduced to another case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.080
from the archives of paranormal investigators&nbsp;
Ed and Lorraine Warren. I personally wouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.920
call any movie that involves ghosts, demons&nbsp;
and witches “a true story” but these events&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:19.840
are based off of real places and people that&nbsp;
Ed and Lorraine encountered in their careers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:23.280
and the film contains hidden references&nbsp;
to some of these real world ideas.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.800
For example, when we see the Warrens’ cursed&nbsp;
artifact room, it contains many paintings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.480
of historic looking houses. These aren’t just&nbsp;
for effect, they actually call back to Ed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:34.160
Lorraine’s early days of investigating. As&nbsp;
the story goes, the two would drive around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:38.080
and Lorraine would use her medium abilities&nbsp;
to detect houses with a supernatural presence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:41.680
Then Ed would create sketches of the house, and&nbsp;
they would go up and offer the artwork to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.040
homeowner, hoping to be invited in, where&nbsp;
they could ease into some questions about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:46.670
possible paranormal activity.

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:50.960
Not you. Ed’s artistic ability comes back and&nbsp;
becomes a major part of The Conjuring 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.160
but before it would, there would be
connections to The Warrens allegedly real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:57.920
case files. To see the truth about the&nbsp;
connection between Bathsheba and Annabelle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:02.080
as well as other disturbing Conjuring secrets,&nbsp;
stick around to the end of this video.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.800
Welcome to Things You Missed. The first two&nbsp;
mainline Conjuring movies came out in 2013&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.200
and 2016 respectively, and since Things You&nbsp;
Missed came out in 2017, I ended up making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:19.280
episodes on all of the Conjuring spin-offs&nbsp;
before I ever did any of the mainline movies.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.960
In a way, that might actually be a good thing,&nbsp;
because the spin-offs are based off of urban&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.320
legends, but they’re more fictionalized,&nbsp;
whereas the main series is adapted from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:30.400
The Warrens’ actual case files. Again, that&nbsp;
doesn’t automatically make it a true story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:35.120
The Warrens CAN make stuff up, and let’s face it&nbsp;
-- they do -- but there is something there. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:40.000
Perrons DID get creeped out by this old house.&nbsp;
The nurses DID become paranoid about this doll.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.160
So putting off these episodes until now gave&nbsp;
me time to read about the cases and find even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:47.920
more Things You might have Missed in the movie,&nbsp;
so with that being said, let’s get into them.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:56.640
The movie begins with Ed and Lorraine&nbsp;
presenting details about the Annabelle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:00.080
case in a lecture hall, and many of their&nbsp;
students are holding a book or pamphlet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:03.440
titled Seekers of the Supernatural. This&nbsp;
isn’t one of their books, but rather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:08.240
the name of an audio program that aired in&nbsp;
1998, 27 years after this scene takes place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:11.280
but maybe this pamphlet was an&nbsp;
early version of this information.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:14.720
You can tell the movie starts in 1971,&nbsp;
not just because of the title right here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.560
but also because of the hard country dish towel&nbsp;
aesthetic of the entire movie. We first meet the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:22.080
Perron family as they are moving into the&nbsp;
property known by most as The Old Arnold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:26.640
Estate. The Conjuring was directed by James Wan,&nbsp;
and moving is a common idea in his movies. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:30.720
2007 movie Dead Silence had the protagonist Jamie&nbsp;
moving out of his old town when he was young,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:34.640
and his 2010 pic, Insidious, focuses on&nbsp;
the Lambert family, who moves in, and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:39.760
quickly moves again, but it turns out, “it’s&nbsp;
not the house that’s haunted, it’s your son.”

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:43.760
The Perrons soon discover the cellar, which we&nbsp;
later find out was the place where Mrs. Walker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:45.600
who lived in the house back in the 1930s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.800
ended her life after her son&nbsp;
Rory disappeared in the woods.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.920
“Nancy, can you go get me the&nbsp;
matches please?” “What’s going on?”

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:54.400
Oh nothing Mom, Dad’s just&nbsp;
gonna burn the house down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:57.840
No, but maybe they WOULD have been better off&nbsp;
just burning the house down. The next morning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.600
their rooms are freezing and the daughter,&nbsp;
Andrea complains of a smell the previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:05.440
night. There are multiple instances of the&nbsp;
characters encountering this smell. Roll it.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:09.440
“That smell again…”

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:12.960
“Andrea was here and she said that&nbsp;
Christine took off with Christine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.040
and April. The girls said she&nbsp;
smelled like rotten meat.”

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:19.360
Which is kind of ironic for a spirit named&nbsp;
BATH-sheba, but the idea to associate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:22.880
foul smell with the haunting may have&nbsp;
come from these books written by Andrea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:26.960
the oldest daughter of the five. She describes&nbsp;
an array of unexplained smells, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:28.960
unlike the movie, not all of them were bad.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:32.640
There was one ghost that Andrea described&nbsp;
as a mother, who “smelled like Ivory soap”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.960
and another who smelled of “flowers and fruit.”&nbsp;
But she also described rancid scents including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:41.120
one that they claimed would hit the house every&nbsp;
morning at 5:15 AM, which I’m guessing was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:45.360
inspiration for another aspect of The Conjuring:&nbsp;
the fact that the clocks stop at the same time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:50.000
every night at exactly 3:07 AM. The 3:07 comes&nbsp;
from the time of death of the supposed witch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.720
Bathsheba Sherman. All in all, the whole move-in&nbsp;
process was a little rockier than expected.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.440
“You still too fried to Christen the&nbsp;
new house?” “Who said I'm fried?”

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:03.520
Dude you already have like 5 daughters, give&nbsp;
it a rest. The wife, Carolyn, wakes up the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:08.800
day with some bruises, and they too are inspired&nbsp;
from real events. Well, not “real”. You know what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:13.280
I mean. In Andrea’s writing, she expresses her&nbsp;
belief that Bathsheba was out to get her mother.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:17.120
“She loathed my mother, she&nbsp;
lusted after my father.”

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:20.720
She claimed there was an incident where the spirit&nbsp;
stabbed Carolyn in the leg with a knitting needle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:24.800
the same weapon she supposedly used to kill&nbsp;
her child before hanging herself on the tree.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:27.680
With The Conjuring, James Wan is&nbsp;
going for a much more subtle horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:30.800
than the crazy events described in&nbsp;
Perron’s books, so in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:35.200
Bathsheba gradually covers her in bruises, making&nbsp;
her think she has some kind of iron deficiency.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:40.000
However, some parts were… not so subtle.&nbsp;
The air yeeting would soon begin.

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:44.938
Yeah, these movies really like the air yeeting.

00:04:46.795 --> 00:04:47.757
But another thing they like to

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:52.320
put in these films is: the easter eggs. As I’ve&nbsp;
discussed with my other James Wan movie analysis’,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:56.240
there is often a hidden image somewhere with&nbsp;
Billy the Puppet from Wan’s breakout film:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:00.320
Saw. The Conjuring is actually the first&nbsp;
Wan movie NOT to feature a cameo from Billy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:03.920
however, there would be a familiar icon&nbsp;
from the Saw franchise. The youngest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:07.600
daughter April discovers this music box,&nbsp;
which features a spinning spiral pattern,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:11.120
which is also the calling card from the&nbsp;
antagonist of the Saw franchise. The box&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:15.600
contains a clown that bounces up and down, whose&nbsp;
design is very very similar to the clown puppet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:21.040
in Wan’s 2007 movie Dead Silence, who fittingly,&nbsp;
also becomes a vessel for a lingering evil spirit.

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:24.960
But it wouldn’t just be Wan’s own creations that&nbsp;
are referenced. The real life Lorraine Warren&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:30.618
served as a consultant for The Conjuring, and she&nbsp;
would make a hidden cameo appearance of her own.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:41.120
In the second lecture scene, Ed and&nbsp;
Lorraine show their audience footage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:45.600
of the possession of a French-Canadian farmer who&nbsp;
once helped Lorraine’s family member in Romania.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:50.160
“Take Maurice here. He’s a French-Canadien farmer,&nbsp;
had nothing more than a 3rd grade education,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:53.520
yet after he was possessed, spoke some&nbsp;
of the best Latin I’d ever heard.”

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:57.520
This is yet another example of something&nbsp;
Andrea claimed to have witnessed. At one point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:00.560
the real Ed and Lorraine attempted to&nbsp;
conduct a seance to try and speak to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:05.040
the dead that lingered in the house, and Andrea&nbsp;
supposedly snuck downstairs to see it. She wrote:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:08.800
“My mother began to speak a language not&nbsp;
of this world in a voice not her own.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:11.440
Her chair levitated and she&nbsp;
was thrown across the room.”

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:14.880
The levitating chair idea was obviously used&nbsp;
in the finale, but they must not have wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:18.320
the foreign tongue idea to go to waste,&nbsp;
and so -- squeezed it into *this scene*&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:22.240
where Ed and Lorraine wowed this paranormally&nbsp;
curious audience. One of these audience members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:26.560
was Lorraine Warren herself, the real Lorraine&nbsp;
Warren, who can be seen sitting in the front row.

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:30.560
As I mentioned, Lorraine was a consultant on the&nbsp;
film who was on set to ensure that the filmmakers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:34.320
were keeping things “historically accurate”.&nbsp;
Whatever the **** that means for a ghost story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:38.160
The funny thing about this, is that it’s basically&nbsp;
more of an honorary role. The script makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:42.160
Lorraine look good, it makes her look like the&nbsp;
hero, and this is probably because the movie is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:46.800
an adaptation of the Warrens’ case files, not an&nbsp;
adaptation of Andrea Perron’s books. My point is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:51.760
that Lorraine has no incentive to step in and&nbsp;
say “oh you know what? I lied. We actually made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:56.080
things worse for them.” Which, according to&nbsp;
the Perron family is basically what happened.

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:00.160
In an interview with The Providence Journal,&nbsp;
the father, Roger, says that his wife’s body was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:03.840
possessed, so the Warrens attempted to perform&nbsp;
a seance to simply try to talk to the spirits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:08.000
in the house, as opposed to the exorcism seen in&nbsp;
the movie where they try to drive Bathsheba out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:11.760
After the seance, Roger says he kicked the&nbsp;
Warrens out, and that was the end of that.

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:16.560
“You get NOTHING! You LOSE! Good day sir!”

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:20.560
They had to keep living in the house until they&nbsp;
could finally afford to move out in the early 80s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:24.480
where they claim they were continually haunted.&nbsp;
Not quite the same as the feel-good ending of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:29.520
the book. Now, did the Warrens really make&nbsp;
things WORSE? No, probably not. This is likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:33.760
an example of confirmation bias. Because the&nbsp;
Perrons already believed in the supernatural,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:37.600
they interpreted everything they saw in the house&nbsp;
as something supernatural, and I think this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:41.600
dramatized in the movie by actually giving&nbsp;
the kids a touch of clairvoyance themselves.

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:44.080
It’s one of the oldest tricks&nbsp;
in the horror genre, kids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:46.560
pets and old people are always&nbsp;
the ones who can see ghosts.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:49.920
When they first arrive at the house, the&nbsp;
dog, Sadie, seems to be the only one who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:54.160
realizes that there are evil spirits lurking&nbsp;
there, refusing to step foot inside. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:58.080
step paw inside. Then there’s the youngest&nbsp;
daughter, April, who tells her Mom that she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:02.000
made friends with a boy named Rory, who we later&nbsp;
learn is one of the kids who formerly lived on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:04.720
the property; the boy who disappeared&nbsp;
in the woods that I mentioned earlier.

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:07.520
The second youngest daughter,&nbsp;
Cindy, is a chronic sleepwalker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:10.400
and at one point the paranormal&nbsp;
investigators determine that she’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:14.400
not just sleepwalking around randomly,&nbsp;
she’s actually being led by a spectre.

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:16.640
“She didn’t trigger it.”
“What do you mean she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:18.320
didn’t trigger it?”
“Somebody’s with her.”

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:22.160
Drew hears Rory’s voice over&nbsp;
EVP saying “Hey, over here.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:25.040
and “Follow me. This is where I hide.”

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:28.240
By the time they get into the room, she’s&nbsp;
disappeared, but they eventually find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:32.160
that her sleepwalk led her into a secret area&nbsp;
behind the wardrobe. It reminds me of this one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:36.000
time when we were kids and my sister fell asleep&nbsp;
behind the futon, it was kind of against the wall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:40.240
but there was a gap. My parents got really&nbsp;
freaked out. But like many elements of this movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:43.360
this too references something from&nbsp;
the Perron sisters’ childhoods.

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:47.120
In the movie, they pass the time by playing&nbsp;
Hide-and-Clap, and in reality they did like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:51.040
to play Hide-and-go-Seek. One of the first times&nbsp;
they played Cindy tried to hide in the woodshed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:55.040
and climbed into this box, but when nobody found&nbsp;
her and she wanted to come out, the lid wouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:59.520
move. With her air supply cut off, she screamed&nbsp;
and cried for 20 minutes until her sister Nancy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:03.920
showed up and easily opened it. There was&nbsp;
no explanation as to why the lid got stuck

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:08.000
So the two youngest seem to have made contact with&nbsp;
the other side. What about the third youngest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:12.560
Christine? In the infamous, figure-behind-the&nbsp;
door scene, Christine is the one who says this…

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:14.400
CHRISTINE: “There’s someone standing over there.”&nbsp;

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:21.520
NANCY: “I don’t see anyone.”
CHRISTINE: “It’s looking right at us.”

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:25.120
What I find interesting about this scene,&nbsp;
as opposed to some of the other scary scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:29.440
where we actually see Bathsheba, is that there’s&nbsp;
actually no one there. I can bring up the lights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:33.680
I can bring down the contrast, it’s just a&nbsp;
dark corner. I think James Wan is telling us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:37.040
by shooting it like this, is that she&nbsp;
can see something that her sister can’t,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:41.040
which is a really unsettling idea, because&nbsp;
it means she had to deal with it alone.

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:44.960
So it seems that these three younger girls&nbsp;
can see some spirits both good and evil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:48.880
while the two older girls and the parents cannot,&nbsp;
and of course Lorraine is the most gifted medium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:52.560
out of any of them because, you know, it’s&nbsp;
based off her writing, she’s gotta be the hero.

00:09:52.728 --> 00:09:54.960
“Supa-hero Lorraine Warren”

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:58.560
There is one other possible example of one&nbsp;
of the two younger girls seeing a horrific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:01.280
event from the past, that you’ll&nbsp;
only pick up on by paying close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:04.560
attention to the small details in&nbsp;
the background of these scenes.

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:06.669
“Maybe it’s just time we take&nbsp;
a break.”

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:19.440
Let’s look at the part where Ed and Lorraine&nbsp;
are researching the grim past of the property.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:22.720
Lorraine shares her findings including&nbsp;
details about several tragedies that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:26.400
have taken place there since the 1860s,&nbsp;
when Bathsheba cursed anyone who would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:31.040
try to take her land. This includes the Walker&nbsp;
family tragedy, a boy who drowned in a pond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:35.280
and a maid who lived on the property. There&nbsp;
is one article she doesn’t mention to Ed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:40.320
but we see it on her desk. The headline reads:&nbsp;
4 Children Killed in Tragic School Bus Accident.

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:43.680
We’ve already seen that the school bus picks&nbsp;
up the Perron girls right at the edge of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:47.520
property, so it makes sense that this area&nbsp;
could be part of Bathsheba’s influence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:50.320
It seems Lorraine found it important&nbsp;
enough to cut out and paste into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:54.320
the book alongside the article about the&nbsp;
pond accident, so it must be significant.

00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:58.320
That brings me to April and Cindy’s room, where&nbsp;
you’ll see a picture of what appears to be two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:02.880
parents and a kid standing in front of a school&nbsp;
bus, with what appears to be a cross in the bottom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:06.480
right corner. It’s not likely that this is a&nbsp;
picture of the Perron sisters going to school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:10.000
because we clearly see that there are four of&nbsp;
them who march off to the bus in the mornings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:14.080
So it could be that the younger sisters have seen&nbsp;
the unrest spirits of this school bus accident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:18.720
in some capacity, the same way Lorraine sees the&nbsp;
gruesome incident taking place in the cellar.

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:21.760
There’s more evidence for this in&nbsp;
the secret area behind the wardrobe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:23.760
Cindy has been known to travel there in her sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:28.160
and when they eventually find her, April&nbsp;
mentions that she knows about the location too.

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:30.560
“That’s where Rory hides when he’s afraid.”

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:34.960
When Lorraine explores it, she finds some toys&nbsp;
on a shelf, including a school bus positioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:39.280
as if it’s crashing into a red pick-up truck.&nbsp;
The shelf below contains an overturned car…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:42.720
It remains unclear if the school bus crash&nbsp;
was at one time supposed to be a bigger part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:46.880
of the story, or if it was always supposed to&nbsp;
be more of a bonus detail for observant fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:50.960
leading our eyes to find more obscure&nbsp;
details hidden away in the children’s rooms.

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:54.640
I also noticed this picture in the bedroom of&nbsp;
Judy, Ed and Lorraine’s young daughter. It appears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:58.480
to be a rooster that says “Mister Wilson”. I’m&nbsp;
not sure if the picture itself is significant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:02.000
but I wonder if the “Mister Wilson” is a&nbsp;
nod to the actor that plays Judy’s father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:06.160
Patrick Wilson. Not the Weezer one. Wilson&nbsp;
is James Wan’s most frequent collaborator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:09.200
having been in five of his movies,&nbsp;
with a sixth currently on the way.

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:11.280
One night when Carolyn hears&nbsp;
a disturbance in the house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:13.840
she wanders downstairs to&nbsp;
investigate when she hears…

00:12:15.560 --> 00:12:16.560
[CLIP]

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:19.120
...which may sound familiar --&nbsp;
these are the same three notes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:23.840
that Carolyn plays when she first&nbsp;
discovers the piano in the cellar.

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:27.440
I had to do some digging, so this&nbsp;
is not my discovery, but rather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:30.720
a trivia bit I found on IMDb. The&nbsp;
piano notes could be a reference to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:35.676
the melody heard in the 1973&nbsp;
movie, The Creeping Flesh.

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:43.280
The Creeping Flesh is a British movie, and&nbsp;
this The Who poster next to the wardrobe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:46.560
in Christine and Nancy’s room contains a&nbsp;
British flag. Could this be a clue about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:51.520
the location of the Warrens’ next big case in&nbsp;
The Conjuring 2 taking place across the pond?

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:55.360
No, probably not. Honestly, probably not.&nbsp;
That’s just looking too much into it.

00:12:55.360 --> 00:13:00.080
BUT, let’s look too much into the camera angles.&nbsp;
The Conjuring uses camera orientation to denote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:03.440
the presence of evil. It first comes up&nbsp;
when Bathsheba’s presence wakes Christine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:06.560
in the middle of the night by pulling her&nbsp;
ankle. She starts by looking under the bed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:10.560
and we see her POV, which is upside down,&nbsp;
as she notices the witch standing beside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:14.480
her door. The camera flips with her as&nbsp;
she sits up to look at the dark corner.

00:13:14.480 --> 00:13:17.920
The technique is used again at the end of the&nbsp;
movie, when Lorraine leaves the basement where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:21.520
Bathsheba is being held to get her bible&nbsp;
so that they can perform the exorcism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:25.040
and the camera flips from upside-down&nbsp;
to right-side up. Then as she returns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:29.280
the camera inverts again, to signify her&nbsp;
return to the lair where the evil resides.

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:33.520
Of course, if you’ve seen the movie, Ed does&nbsp;
successfully perform that exorcism. And if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:36.800
haven’t seen the movie, I seriously don’t know&nbsp;
what you’re doing on this video, and this far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:41.200
into it to boot, but before we wrap this one up,&nbsp;
there’s one more big mystery I want to hit on.

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:44.240
The Conjuring would be followed up&nbsp;
one year later by the first spin-off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:47.760
Annabelle, which is teased at with this&nbsp;
commercial, I mean scene, in The Conjuring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:50.720
where the doll escapes and nearly&nbsp;
attacks Ed and Lorraine’s daughter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:54.800
Judy. But how is this evil entity able&nbsp;
to escape from a super locked case in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.800 --> 00:13:59.040
super locked room? The details in the scene and&nbsp;
the scenes leading up to it, have the answer.

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:02.960
First, let’s take it back to when Ed is&nbsp;
explaining the situation to the Perrons. Roll it

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:09.760
“Sometimes when you get haunted, it's like&nbsp;
stepping on gum. You can take it with you.”

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:13.200
By these rules, it stands to reason that&nbsp;
our paranormal investigators could also be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.200 --> 00:14:17.040
victims of this, and during Lorraine’s&nbsp;
investigations something grabs her locket,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:21.040
with the picture of Judy in it. This shows&nbsp;
Bathsheba who Lorraine cares about the most,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:24.000
so Bathsheba shows Lorraine an&nbsp;
image of her daughter in danger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:28.400
which cause Lorraine go back and check on her,&nbsp;
unknowingly leading the witch to her home.

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:32.720
Then, one night while Judy is asleep, it’s&nbsp;
her copy of the locket that starts shaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:35.760
before she wakes up, while&nbsp;
the clock reads 3:07 AM,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:39.840
the same time that Bathsheba always caused the&nbsp;
clocks to stop at in the Old Arnold Estate.

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:44.080
You shouldn’t need any further proof, that&nbsp;
Bathsheba was the one to free Malthus, the spirit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:48.640
inside of the Annabelle doll, but if you do, look&nbsp;
no further than Judy’s recount of the events.

00:14:48.640 --> 00:14:52.480
“There was someone rocking&nbsp;
in the chair with Annabelle.”

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:57.120
Judy also has some clairvoyant abilities, as we&nbsp;
see in The Conjuring 2 and Annabelle Comes Home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.120 --> 00:14:59.680
so this is her saying that the&nbsp;
inhuman entity residing in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:03.120
Annabelle doll wasn’t the only thing&nbsp;
she sensed. Bathsheba had tried to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:06.560
scare The Warrens off of her case&nbsp;
by going after her young daughter.

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:10.400
The Annabelle case is one of 1968 is&nbsp;
one of the most well known Warren cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.400 --> 00:15:14.000
but there is one even more famous one,&nbsp;
that is referenced at the end of the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:17.040
when Ed and Lorraine return home&nbsp;
and touch base with Father Gordon.

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:19.920
“If we can, he’d like to meet with us tomorrow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:22.480
There’s a case in Long&nbsp;
Island he’d like to discuss.”

00:15:22.480 --> 00:15:26.000
She's talking about Long Island, New York,&nbsp;
where the infamous haunting of the The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:30.560
Amityville Horror takes place, which leads us&nbsp;
right up to the beginning of The Conjuring 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:33.840
where Ed and Lorraine investigate&nbsp;
that very case in the film’s opening.

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:37.120
There is a movie entirely dedicated&nbsp;
to The Amityville Horror, I’m sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.120 --> 00:15:39.280
you can guess what it’s called,&nbsp;
but that film is not a part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:42.080
the series of movies that we like&nbsp;
to refer to as “The Conjurverse.”

00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:45.120
And for now, we’ve only got one more&nbsp;
movie to cover in The Conjurverse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:48.560
and that is The Conjuring 2, which&nbsp;
happens to be my favorite of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:51.520
franchise and it’s loaded with&nbsp;
hidden messages and secrets.

